# Flows SDK

Developer library for programatically creating Hyperscience Flows.


## Local development

Create a venv, source it and install requirements-dev.txt
(note that requirements-dev uses the `-e` flag that allows for alive-reload of changes during dev/testing)

```
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```

#### Folder structure

- `/flows_sdk`- the package we deliver as "flows-sdk". Everything inside will be part of the distributed .whl
  - `/flows_sdk/implementations`- bundles of block definitions specific to a particular Hyperscience release
- `/docs`- root for the documentation, check out [Docs structure](#docs-structure) below for details
- `/samples`- example e2e flows that are distributed as part of the documentation. Check out [Docs structure](#docs-structure) below for details
- `/media` - misc files used in this readme(e.g. images, drawio source, slack workflow source)
- `/tests` - unit tests root


#### Running code formatters

At this time, only pylint and mypy are enforced and code formatters are recommended, but not CI checked.

From the root git folder, run isort and black in that particular order:

`isort -rc . && black .` (or execute `run_code_format.sh`)

- isort config can be found as `.isort.cfg`
- black config can be found as `pyproject.toml`

#### Pylint

`run_pylint.sh`

- pylint config is under `.pylintrc`

#### Mypy

`run_mypy.sh`

- mypy config is under `mypy.ini`

## Release

We use both Github Actions (under `.github`) and CircleCI (under `.circle`) for CI automation.

Example valid release, reachable from HS infrastructure only:
`https://build-storage.int.hyperscience.com/flows_sdk/v0.4.1/flows_sdk-0.4.1-py3-none-any.whl`

To release a new version of the library, create a PR that also updates the version in `VERSION.txt`.
We use [semantic versioning](https://semver.org/).
A new version includes all commits between the previous version and the updated one.

Once you merge a PR into master, a Github Action will try to create a TAG for the commit. 
This will be skipped if there is already an existing tag for the same version (e.g., version `1.2.3` and tag `v1.2.3`).
Once the tag is applied, CircleCI will build and release the library under `https://{s3-url}/flows_sdk/{tag}/` as both a `.whl` wheel and `.tar.gz` sources.

Manual tagging is also allowed in case a custom version needs to be created (e.g. `1.2.3-experimental`).

![](media/git.png)

Note: you can also build a wheel file locally using `python -m build`. 
This will build a `.whl` and `.tar.gz` of the current version under `{repo-dir}/dist`

#### Slack notifications

On a new version in `master`, a slack notification will be sent. 
This happens in  [in the main github workflow](.github/workflows/main.yml) by checking if a new tag has been applied and 
triggering a slack workflow via webhook when that happens.

The definition of the slack workflow can be found in [media/flows_sdk_new_release_notification.slackworkflow](media/flows_sdk_new_release_notification.slackworkflow)


## Docs

For documenting this library we use [Sphinx](https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/index.html).

We use [`autodoc`](https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/extensions/autodoc.html)
for generating sources documentation from docstrings. As a rule of thumb, all non-private
classes/functions should have docstrings in reStructured format

### Releasing the docs to https://flows-sdk.hyperscience.com/
[AWS Amplify](https://us-east-1.console.aws.amazon.com/amplify/home?region=us-east-1#/d1nw87xs6xnvpy) 
(under the `hs-prod` account)
continuously delivers the documentation generated from the **branch** `flows-sdk.hyperscience.com`.
In order to release a new version of the documentation, open a PR from `master` to `flows-sdk.hyperscience.com`. 
Once merged, the docs will be built and deployed, reachable within a couple of minutes on the public website.

NOTE: Please use the "rebase" merging option in order to preserve history parity between `master` and `flows-sdk.hypersciece.com`.
For all other commits targeting `master`, we prefer the "Squash & Merge" option. 

### Building the docs
In an environment with installed `requirements-dev.txt`, run `./run_build_docs.sh` to build
and bundle the mix of static pages and autogenerated from docstrings docs.

### Serving locally
To locally preview the generated documentation, you can use any HTTP server. For example,
from the base dir invoke pythons' built-in server and point it to `docs/build`:
`python -m http.server --directory docs/build`

If you are actively editing, you can use the `./run_local_editing.sh` script that rebuilds the documentation
and starts a local server on http://localhost:8989.

### Docs structure
Documentation sources live under `docs/source` and the build results in static files under `docs/build`.

Notable files and folders:
- `docs/source/conf.py` - sphinx configuration
- `docs/source/index.rst` - used to generate the `index.html` initial page and define the "toctree"
(table of content) from the pages under `/pages`
- `docs/source/pages` - a mix of `.md` and `.rst` static files that make up the documentation.
Note that "heading1" produces top-level links in the toctree, so as a convention, every file should have
a single "heading1" (but can have as many h2, h3, etc. that result in nested links/anchors)
- `docs/source/pages/examples.rst` - we showcase some of the examples under `/samples` as a preview-able
python code and downloadable starters. Because `sphinx` cannot make "folders" downloadable,
when there are multiple files, we first archive the folder and then add that archive to the static
docs (check out `./run_build_docs.sh`)

### Doc writing tips & tricks

Links in markdown, depending on the context:
- external link (regular .md syntax) [some text](https://hyperscience.com)
- deeplink within page [some text](page.md#the-anchor)[1]
- link to code documentation that has been included in "autodoc" [some text](flows_sdk.blocks.Block)

[1] anchors are automatically generated for all h1 and h2 headings, 
controlled by `myst_heading_anchors = 2 ` that is set in`conf.py`

### Examples
Examples are self-contained flows that can be built and deployed on a Hyperscience instance.
They target a particular platform version (e.g. v33).

Some of the IDP examples include a pre-made layout release alongside an accompanying document,
allowing users to easily test them end-to-end as a starting point.

Examples live under the `/samples` folder. Every example is a self-contained folder, suffixed by
the Hyperscience platform version that the example is targeting (e.g. `idp_flow_v33`).

During execution of `./run_build_docs.sh`, all folders are zipped into archives with the same name (e.g. `idp_flow_v33.zip`).
Those zips can be referenced in the documentation - that's how we construct the download links in the examples page (`/docs/source/pages/examples.rst`)


## CHANGELOG

For every version, regardless if major, minor or patch, there should be a description of all changes in the [CHANGELOG.md](docs/source/pages/CHANGELOG.md).
There should be a separate "heading 2" section in the CHANGELOG with the version number (`##` in markdown, e.g. `## 1.0.1`).
The versions should be in descending order (the latest version on top of the CHANGELOG).

Every (meaningful) change should be tagged with a prefix, depending on the type of change:
- `[feature]` for new features, some new functionality that previously did not exist
    * examples of features include: adding a new block, introducing new functionality to the SDK
- `[bugfix]` for undesired behavior that was fixed as part of that version
    * examples of bugfixes include: fixing a blocks' inputs, fixing a broken example
- `[improvement]` for general improvements in usability, UX, not related to undesired behavior, but improved nonetheless.
    * example of improvement include: adding new examples, making internal methods easier to use
- `[deprecated]` for features that we plan to remove. Note that unless of platform incompatibilities,
we should keep deprecated features around until the next major version!
- `[removed]` for deprecated features that were removed
